program            = 004_case_ranges
area               = 08_gcc_extensions
description        = GCC case ranges (case LOW ... HIGH:) including negative, character, degenerate single-value and fallthrough ranges
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_audit_flags     = -Wall -Wextra -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror
ub_notes           <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them at -O0, -O1 and -O2. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0 and re-verified on this branch with gcc 13.4.0. The canonical record of this
deviation is the matching paragraph of impl_defined_notes below, which is the one field
this suite reads a gate reason from; it is restated here so that the whole argument for
this program's fitness as test material can be read in one place.

UB-FREEDOM ARGUMENT. Every value is a small int; the largest computation is 1 + 10, far
from INT_MIN and INT_MAX, so there is no signed overflow. No shift operator is used. No
pointer, array or aggregate is used, so there is no aliasing violation and no
one-past-end pointer. Every object is initialized before it is read. No object is
modified twice between sequence points. Every printf call receives at most one
side-effecting argument. Nothing depends on padding bytes or on the addresses of
unrelated objects. Every switch has a default label, so control flow is total for every
possible operand value, and the case ranges within any one switch never overlap, so no
label is ambiguous. The sweep loop runs from -25 to 35 inclusive and always terminates.

Every switch operand is int, never plain char. The character constants used as range
bounds have type int in C. This is deliberate: char was measured as signed on x86_64 and
i686 but unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64, so a plain-char operand could change which
range matches on different targets and manufacture a divergence caused by the test rather
than by the compiler.

The deliberate fallthrough between two case ranges carries a /* fall through */ comment,
which GCC recognizes to suppress -Wimplicit-fallthrough; -Wextra enables that warning and
-Werror makes it fatal, so the comment is required for the gate to pass.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
UB-GATE DEVIATION AND ITS REASON. Every program in area 08_gcc_extensions drops
-pedantic from the default warning gate. Reason: the constructs under test are
non-standard GCC extensions by definition, and -pedantic exists precisely to reject
them; retaining it would fail every program in the area for doing exactly what it is
meant to do. This is the only area-wide deviation in the corpus. No other warning is
relaxed: -Wall, -Wextra, -Wconversion, -Wsign-conversion, -Wshadow and -Werror are all
retained, and this program is clean under them at -O0, -O1 and -O2. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0 and re-verified on this branch with gcc 13.4.0. A gate deviation is not a
divergence: the gate is a property of the TEST MATERIAL, it is driven by the reference
compiler only, and it renders no verdict about bcc.

Nothing implementation-defined is observed, so no target restriction is needed and all
four targets are compared. Only int values are printed, so the measured width differences
cannot be reached: sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) are 4 on i686 and 8 on x86_64, aarch64
and riscv64. Plain char is never used as a switch operand or a printed value, so the
measured char-signedness difference cannot be reached. No address or pointer value is
printed.

Case range syntax requires a space on both sides of the ellipsis. Measured with
gcc 13.3.0 and re-verified with gcc 13.4.0: writing case 1...5: without spaces produces
"error: too many decimal points in number", because the lexer reads 1. followed by ..5 as
a malformed floating constant. Every range in the program keeps the spaces.

The runtime probes use volatile int operands so the optimizer cannot fold the switch away
and must emit a genuine lowering. Dense and sparse label sets select different lowering
strategies, a jump table versus a comparison chain, and only the runtime variant
exercises them.

Output is identical on all four targets at all three optimization levels.
END
expected_divergence.id       = XD-GCCEXT-CASE-RANGES-001
expected_divergence.class    = compile_failure
expected_divergence.scope    = oracle_a; all targets; all opt levels
expected_divergence.basis    = docs/technical-specifications.md, documented GCC extension inventory omits case ranges -- line 13, line 107 and line 761
expected_divergence.observed <<END
bcc is expected to reject the case range syntax at the case label, because case ranges
are absent from every documented bcc extension inventory. The reference compiler accepts
the program, links it and prints the 18 recorded lines beginning cr_bucket_neg=10.

Basis detail. The documented inventory appears at docs/technical-specifications.md lines
13, 107 and 761 and at docs/project-guide.md lines 81 and 206. The three technical
specification entries each enumerate the same seven extensions -- __attribute__,
__builtin_* intrinsics, inline assembly with operand constraints, statement expressions,
typeof and __typeof__, computed goto, and __extension__ -- and the two project guide
entries enumerate five of those same seven. Every one of the five omits case ranges. A
case-insensitive grep for "case range" across docs/ returns zero matches.

Provenance, stated so the marker is not mistaken for an observation. This branch carries
the corpus but no bcc binary, so no verdict about bcc has been recorded for these cells
and the rejection above is stated as anticipated rather than as already seen. The first
run in which bcc itself compiles this program settles it: a rejection is the XFAIL this
marker describes, and an acceptance is the XPASS retirement path below.

Per constraint C3 the program is written and executed regardless: this marker changes how
a divergence is CLASSIFIED, never whether the feature is EXERCISED. The feature is not
silently excluded from testing.

Scope note for maintainers. This marker covers oracle_a only, and classification is
strict: it will not absorb a divergence of a different class or a different oracle. If bcc
instead ACCEPTS case ranges but computes a wrong result, that is a stdout_mismatch and not
a compile_failure, so this marker will not cover it and the divergence will be reported as
a FINDING, which is the correct outcome. A compile_failure also denies the cross-backend
and golden-record arms the subject of their comparison, and those arms fall outside an
oracle_a scope, so they are reported as FINDING too; widening the scope to all oracles, in
this record and in the register together, is the one-word change that would absorb them.

Retirement. If bcc compiles and runs this program correctly, the divergence has
disappeared and the verdict becomes XPASS, which FAILS THE RUN by default. A stale marker
is stale documented knowledge, and retiring it is a trivial test-only edit: delete all
five expected_divergence keys from this record AND delete the corresponding entry from
tests/conformance/EXPECTED_DIVERGENCES.md. Both directions are machine-checked. This
ambiguity is also flagged for a maintainer to confirm whether the omission is an
implementation gap or a documentation gap.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
cr_bucket_neg=10
cr_bucket_b1=10
cr_bucket_b2=10
cr_bucket_b3=1
cr_bucket_b4=10
cr_bucket_default=20
cr_hex_digit=7
cr_hex_lower=14
cr_hex_upper=11
cr_hex_none=-1
cr_single_hit=42
cr_single_miss=0
cr_fall_low=11
cr_fall_mid=10
cr_fall_high=100
cr_fall_none=0
cr_runtime_bucket=2
cr_runtime_hex=12
END
